1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,152
Welcome to GlucoTwin India, an adaptive partial digital twin for glucose forecasting. This project

2
00:00:06,152 --> 00:00:12,303
is submitted by Dipanshu Raj, Team product, from the Indian Institute of Technology Kanpur,

3
00:00:12,303 --> 00:00:18,455
for the Digital Twin Challenge 2026. The prototype joins the same study participant's baseline

4
00:00:18,455 --> 00:00:24,606
clinical profile with a replay of their glucose, activity and recorded meal data. It

5
00:00:24,606 --> 00:00:30,758
maintains an evolving patient state, generates forecasts, and makes its uncertainty and limitations visible.

6
00:00:30,758 --> 00:00:36,909
In this demonstration, we will examine the clinical question, the data, the architecture, the

7
00:00:36,909 --> 00:00:43,061
evaluation and the working dashboard. The results come from real, openly available research data.

8
00:00:43,061 --> 00:00:49,212
The narration in this version is synthesized. This is a research demonstration, not a

9
00:00:49,212 --> 00:00:55,364
clinically validated medical device. Its strongest measured result is improved glucose forecasting; its exploratory

10
00:00:55,364 --> 00:00:58,000
event alerts still require substantial improvement.

11
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:06,240
The intended use case is a clinician reviewing an adult with Type 2 diabetes

12
00:01:06,240 --> 00:01:12,480
between periodic consultations. The narrow question is whether clinical context and recent sensor history

13
00:01:12,480 --> 00:01:18,720
can improve the forecast of glucose in the next hour. We also evaluate thirty-minute

14
00:01:18,720 --> 00:01:24,960
and two-hour horizons. A forecast horizon is the time between issuing a prediction and

15
00:01:24,960 --> 00:01:31,200
the value being predicted. It must not be confused with the amount of advance

16
00:01:31,200 --> 00:01:37,440
warning actually achieved before an event. Our exploratory event definition requires a new crossing

17
00:01:37,440 --> 00:01:43,680
above one hundred eighty milligrams per deciliter, followed by three consecutive five-minute bins above

18
00:01:43,680 --> 00:01:49,920
that threshold. The whole event must fit inside the next sixty minutes. This persistence

19
00:01:49,920 --> 00:01:56,160
rule is an engineering endpoint for the experiment. It is not an emergency diagnosis

20
00:01:56,160 --> 00:02:02,400
or an individualized treatment target. The prototype outputs information for review. It does not

21
00:02:02,400 --> 00:02:08,640
prescribe insulin, change medication, or claim that a forecast will prevent a complication. Keeping

22
00:02:08,640 --> 00:02:14,880
the question narrow makes the result testable and keeps the demonstration aligned with the

23
00:02:14,880 --> 00:02:18,000
challenge's requirement for one localized health outcome.

24
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:27,000
Our primary source is the openly available CGMacros version one dataset from PhysioNet. It

25
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:34,000
contains forty-five participants, including fourteen in the study's Type 2 diabetes group. Baseline demographics

26
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:41,000
and laboratory measurements are joined to glucose, wearable activity and meal information using the

27
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:48,000
same participant identifier. We represent the baseline fields as an EHR-like clinical record. We

28
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:55,000
do not claim a working connection to a hospital EHR. We also do not

29
00:02:55,000 --> 00:03:02,000
attach an unrelated synthetic medical record to a real sensor trace. That would demonstrate

30
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:09,000
a software join, but it would not establish a meaningful patient-level relationship. The source

31
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:16,000
study was conducted in the United States, so the intended Indian application remains unvalidated.

32
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:23,000
The dataset is licensed under Creative Commons Attribution, NonCommercial, ShareAlike four point zero. Source

33
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:30,000
files, processed derivatives, citations and license notices are included in the repository. Meal photographs

34
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:37,000
and microbiome data are excluded because this implementation does not use them. The package

35
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:44,000
preserves the forty-five participant CSV files and the baseline table, together with source checksums,

36
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:48,000
so an evaluator can rebuild the processed data.

37
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:57,422
The architecture begins with two patient-linked inputs. The static branch contains age, recorded sex,

38
00:03:57,422 --> 00:04:04,843
body mass index, glycated hemoglobin and fasting glucose. The dynamic branch contains glucose history,

39
00:04:04,843 --> 00:04:12,265
heart rate, activity intensity and recorded meal macronutrients. The preparation stage aligns timestamps, records

40
00:04:12,265 --> 00:04:19,687
missingness, and applies the conservative input timing policy. Causal feature construction then creates rolling

41
00:04:19,687 --> 00:04:27,108
glucose summaries, trends, decaying meal states and activity summaries. A separate direct regression model

42
00:04:27,108 --> 00:04:34,530
predicts each forecast horizon. The patient-specific residual state is updated only when the outcome

43
00:04:34,530 --> 00:04:41,952
of an earlier prediction has become observable. This feedback loop is the adaptive part

44
00:04:41,952 --> 00:04:49,373
of the twin. Independent calibration data supply residual interval widths. A separate classifier supplies

45
00:04:49,373 --> 00:04:56,795
an exploratory event ranking score. A data-quality gate determines whether the dashboard is allowed

46
00:04:56,795 --> 00:05:04,217
to show a forecast. The frontend presents the profile, the trace, the forecast, the

47
00:05:04,217 --> 00:05:11,639
interval and the review state. A separate scenario sandbox illustrates hypothetical response assumptions. It

48
00:05:11,639 --> 00:05:18,000
is outside the evaluated forecasting pathway and cannot change the published results.

49
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:27,416
Time ordering is central to this project. The source data already contain glucose values

50
00:05:27,416 --> 00:05:34,832
interpolated to one-minute intervals. Treating all of those values as independent real-time observations would

51
00:05:34,832 --> 00:05:42,249
overstate what was available at a prediction timestamp. We therefore retain readings compatible with

52
00:05:42,249 --> 00:05:49,665
the initial five-minute Dexcom phase, discard fractional values, and avoid adding any backward interpolation.

53
00:05:49,665 --> 00:05:57,081
All dynamic inputs are delayed by fifteen minutes. A single missing input bin may

54
00:05:57,081 --> 00:06:04,497
be carried forward, but target values are not filled. This policy is conservative, yet

55
00:06:04,497 --> 00:06:11,914
it cannot fully reconstruct the unavailable native device exports. That remaining limitation is stated

56
00:06:11,914 --> 00:06:19,330
throughout the submission. For personalization, consider a forecast issued for sixty minutes ahead. With

57
00:06:19,330 --> 00:06:26,746
a fifteen-minute observation delay, its error becomes usable seventy-five minutes later. The model must

58
00:06:26,746 --> 00:06:34,162
wait until that time before updating its residual memory. Our correction uses a fixed,

59
00:06:34,162 --> 00:06:41,578
bounded, exponentially weighted state. Unit tests check that changing future inputs cannot alter earlier

60
00:06:41,578 --> 00:06:48,995
features, and that forecasts recomputed from a prefix agree with the corresponding portion of

61
00:06:48,995 --> 00:06:56,411
a whole-trace computation. These tests support the implementation's causal timing; they do not certify

62
00:06:56,411 --> 00:06:58,000
the upstream dataset.

63
00:07:00,000 --> 00:07:06,960
The evaluation uses four disjoint participant partitions. Thirty-six participants are used for training. Three

64
00:07:06,960 --> 00:07:13,921
different Type 2 diabetes participants are used for validation, three for interval calibration, and

65
00:07:13,921 --> 00:07:20,881
three for final testing. The split uses a fixed random seed and its exact

66
00:07:20,881 --> 00:07:27,842
identifiers are published. Validation selects between the fused and adaptive variants and determines the

67
00:07:27,842 --> 00:07:34,802
alert threshold. Calibration determines the empirical residual interval widths. The test participants do not

68
00:07:34,802 --> 00:07:41,763
select the models, the thresholds or the interval widths. The first twenty-four hours of

69
00:07:41,763 --> 00:07:48,723
each trace are treated as warm-up and excluded from scored windows. Earlier test-patient observations

70
00:07:48,723 --> 00:07:55,684
may update the patient state, but future observations cannot. We compare persistence, a sensor-only

71
00:07:55,684 --> 00:08:02,644
model, a fused clinical and sensor model, and the adaptive variant. Persistence simply carries

72
00:08:02,644 --> 00:08:09,605
the most recent available glucose forward and is a meaningful baseline. Headline errors average

73
00:08:09,605 --> 00:08:16,565
each patient's result equally. This avoids allowing a longer trace to dominate the result.

74
00:08:16,565 --> 00:08:23,525
Overlapping windows remain temporally dependent, and the final test contains only three people, so

75
00:08:23,525 --> 00:08:28,000
the results support feasibility rather than broad clinical generalization.

76
00:08:30,000 --> 00:08:36,659
The chart shows measured mean absolute error in milligrams per deciliter on the held-out

77
00:08:36,659 --> 00:08:43,319
test participants. At thirty minutes, persistence has an error of nineteen point seven four

78
00:08:43,319 --> 00:08:49,978
and the adaptive twin has eighteen point four eight. At sixty minutes, the error

79
00:08:49,978 --> 00:08:56,638
decreases from twenty-six point six seven to twenty-four point one two, an improvement of

80
00:08:56,638 --> 00:09:03,297
approximately nine point six percent. At one hundred twenty minutes, it decreases from thirty-five

81
00:09:03,297 --> 00:09:09,957
point two six to twenty-nine point zero zero, an improvement of approximately seventeen point

82
00:09:09,957 --> 00:09:16,616
seven percent. These are relative reductions in mean absolute error, not classification accuracy and

83
00:09:16,616 --> 00:09:23,276
not demonstrated patient benefit. The fused model without adaptation does not outperform persistence at

84
00:09:23,276 --> 00:09:29,935
every horizon. We publish that result rather than attribute every improvement to clinical features.

85
00:09:29,935 --> 00:09:36,595
The strongest variant in this experiment combines fused forecasting with delayed residual adaptation. At

86
00:09:36,595 --> 00:09:43,254
the sixty-minute horizon, intervals designed for nominal ninety percent coverage achieved approximately eighty-nine point

87
00:09:43,254 --> 00:09:49,914
eight percent patient-macro test coverage. Their typical width is substantial, which is important for

88
00:09:49,914 --> 00:09:56,573
interpreting any individual forecast. All per-patient values and the other baselines remain available in

89
00:09:56,573 --> 00:09:58,000
the results files.

90
00:10:00,000 --> 00:10:07,000
We now examine the working local dashboard. The participant selector changes both the clinical

91
00:10:07,000 --> 00:10:14,000
profile and the sensor stream. These are held-out study participants with anonymized identifiers, not

92
00:10:14,000 --> 00:10:21,000
invented patient identities. The first value is the latest available glucose, together with its

93
00:10:21,000 --> 00:10:28,000
age. The second is the sixty-minute forecast and its estimated interval. The third is

94
00:10:28,000 --> 00:10:35,000
the event model's ranking score. Because the classifier was trained with class balancing, this

95
00:10:35,000 --> 00:10:42,000
score is explicitly not presented as a clinical probability. The review state describes whether

96
00:10:42,000 --> 00:10:49,000
the data are sufficient and whether the score crosses the validation-selected review threshold. In

97
00:10:49,000 --> 00:10:56,000
the trajectory chart, the observed history is shown in teal. Forecast points are shown

98
00:10:56,000 --> 00:11:03,000
in purple, with a shaded uncertainty region. The vertical line marks the forecast issue

99
00:11:03,000 --> 00:11:10,000
time. The latest input is intentionally earlier because of the observation delay. Pressing the

100
00:11:10,000 --> 00:11:17,000
step control advances the replay by five minutes; the play control repeatedly advances it.

101
00:11:17,000 --> 00:11:24,000
The patient state and forecasts are recomputed as the replay advances. The observed future

102
00:11:24,000 --> 00:11:31,000
is hidden by default. Enabling the reveal control overlays the subsequently observed trace for

103
00:11:31,000 --> 00:11:38,000
retrospective evaluation. This is a demonstration aid, not information supplied to the prediction model.

104
00:11:38,000 --> 00:11:45,000
The export button creates a JSON review record containing the displayed context, forecast, interval

105
00:11:45,000 --> 00:11:52,000
and quality state. The following sequence records real interactions with this prototype. Aggregate evaluation

106
00:11:52,000 --> 00:11:58,000
is reported separately, because a selected demonstration example cannot establish model performance.

107
00:12:00,000 --> 00:12:06,844
The episode-alert results are intentionally shown separately from glucose forecasting. On the final test

108
00:12:06,844 --> 00:12:13,689
set, sixty-four alerts were issued under the defined cooldown policy. Fourteen matched an upcoming

109
00:12:13,689 --> 00:12:20,533
observable onset, and fifty were false alerts. Fourteen of sixty-six observable onsets were detected.

110
00:12:20,533 --> 00:12:27,378
This corresponds to approximately twenty-one point nine percent precision and twenty-one point two percent

111
00:12:27,378 --> 00:12:34,222
recall. False alerts occurred at approximately one point eight seven per eligible patient-day. The

112
00:12:34,222 --> 00:12:41,067
median observed advance warning among matched alerts was fifteen minutes. These numbers show that

113
00:12:41,067 --> 00:12:47,911
the current event-ranking module is not ready for clinical reliance. They also explain why

114
00:12:47,911 --> 00:12:54,756
we do not advertise reliable two-hour event prediction. The two-hour model forecasts a glucose

115
00:12:54,756 --> 00:13:01,600
value; the alert experiment has a different endpoint and a shorter observed warning. An

116
00:13:01,600 --> 00:13:08,444
alert is matched to at most one onset, repeated alerts are limited by a

117
00:13:08,444 --> 00:13:15,289
sixty-minute cooldown, and the full three-bin event must fit in the prediction window. The

118
00:13:15,289 --> 00:13:22,133
denominator for false alerts is eligible monitored time. Publishing these definitions makes the result

119
00:13:22,133 --> 00:13:28,000
auditable and prevents an attractive dashboard from concealing an immature alarm system.

120
00:13:30,000 --> 00:13:36,696
A useful research dashboard must show when the model should not produce an answer.

121
00:13:36,696 --> 00:13:43,391
Here we activate the sensor-outage control. The forecast and event score are withheld, the

122
00:13:43,391 --> 00:13:50,087
review state changes to insufficient data, and the interface asks for fresh sensor information.

123
00:13:50,087 --> 00:13:56,783
This is a deterministic stress demonstration of the quality gate, not a real connection

124
00:13:56,783 --> 00:14:03,478
being disconnected. In the underlying pipeline, forecasts are suppressed when the latest glucose is

125
00:14:03,478 --> 00:14:10,174
missing, the sensor is too stale, there is insufficient recent history, or the warm-up

126
00:14:10,174 --> 00:14:16,870
period is incomplete. The last observed value can still be displayed as historical context,

127
00:14:16,870 --> 00:14:23,565
but it is not represented as a fresh measurement. Restoring the stream makes the

128
00:14:23,565 --> 00:14:30,261
forecast available again when the quality conditions are satisfied. Other controls separate retrospective future

129
00:14:30,261 --> 00:14:36,957
reveal from normal review and label an already high observed glucose differently from a

130
00:14:36,957 --> 00:14:43,652
predicted future onset. These distinctions matter: a current-state flag must not be counted as

131
00:14:43,652 --> 00:14:50,348
an advance warning. The unit tests cover output suppression, future-data hiding, invalid participant selection

132
00:14:50,348 --> 00:14:57,043
and prediction-prefix consistency. Browser checks also exercise replay, tabs, scenario controls, participant switching and

133
00:14:57,043 --> 00:14:58,000
mobile layout.

134
00:15:00,000 --> 00:15:06,960
The scenario page is an illustration of what a future validated physiological twin could

135
00:15:06,960 --> 00:15:13,921
support. It is deliberately separate from the forecasting models. The user can change a

136
00:15:13,921 --> 00:15:20,881
hypothetical carbohydrate amount, an activity duration, and an assumed response gain. A simple impulse-response

137
00:15:20,881 --> 00:15:27,842
equation then generates a synthetic curve. These coefficients are assumptions. They are not estimated

138
00:15:27,842 --> 00:15:34,802
causal effects of food or exercise for the selected participant. Changing a slider therefore

139
00:15:34,802 --> 00:15:41,763
cannot justify a recommendation to change that person's meal, activity, insulin or medication. The

140
00:15:41,763 --> 00:15:48,723
comparison is useful for explaining model assumptions and for testing interface behavior. It is

141
00:15:48,723 --> 00:15:55,684
not evidence of treatment efficacy. The scenario does not modify the patient's stored history,

142
00:15:55,684 --> 00:16:02,644
the trained forecasting models or the evaluation results. A future clinical version would need

143
00:16:02,644 --> 00:16:09,605
an independently validated physiological model, intervention data, safeguards and clinician supervision before presenting treatment

144
00:16:09,605 --> 00:16:16,565
comparisons. For this challenge submission, the sandbox provides a transparent conceptual interaction while the

145
00:16:16,565 --> 00:16:23,525
real-data forecasting experiment remains the evidence-bearing component. This boundary is visible on the page,

146
00:16:23,525 --> 00:16:28,000
in the model card and in the technical report.

147
00:16:30,000 --> 00:16:36,588
India is the intended healthcare context, but the current data are from a small

148
00:16:36,588 --> 00:16:43,176
United States study. We therefore describe a deployment pathway rather than claim Indian clinical

149
00:16:43,176 --> 00:16:49,765
performance. The first step is access to appropriately governed native glucose and clinical records

150
00:16:49,765 --> 00:16:56,353
from representative Indian participants. The next step is a preregistered external evaluation, including local

151
00:16:56,353 --> 00:17:02,941
meal patterns, sensor availability, missingness, age and other relevant subgroups. Event risk would need

152
00:17:02,941 --> 00:17:09,529
better discrimination and probability calibration. Clinicians would need to help define useful review thresholds

153
00:17:09,529 --> 00:17:16,118
and acceptable alert burden. A prospective silent evaluation should come before visible clinical alerts,

154
00:17:16,118 --> 00:17:22,706
so that performance can be measured without changing treatment. Only after those steps would

155
00:17:22,706 --> 00:17:29,294
a larger pilot and applicable regulatory assessment be appropriate. The local prototype has no

156
00:17:29,294 --> 00:17:35,882
paid model API dependency and can run after setup without a network connection. That

157
00:17:35,882 --> 00:17:42,471
is an engineering property, not proof that continuous glucose monitoring is affordable or universally

158
00:17:42,471 --> 00:17:49,059
available. No cost savings, improved clinical outcomes or regulatory compliance certification is claimed. The

159
00:17:49,059 --> 00:17:55,647
research package makes the remaining work explicit so the next stage can evaluate a

160
00:17:55,647 --> 00:17:58,000
concrete and realistic development plan.

161
00:18:00,000 --> 00:18:06,064
The implementation uses Python, NumPy, pandas, SciPy and scikit-learn. The browser frontend uses plain

162
00:18:06,064 --> 00:18:12,127
HTML, CSS and JavaScript, with no external runtime scripts. A standard-library server binds to

163
00:18:12,127 --> 00:18:18,191
localhost. The package includes the source CSV subset, processed data, the pretrained model, exact

164
00:18:18,191 --> 00:18:24,255
participant splits, evaluation outputs and twelve tests. First-time installation requires internet for dependencies, but

165
00:18:24,255 --> 00:18:30,318
the demonstration then runs locally without an API key or GPU. Training took approximately

166
00:18:30,318 --> 00:18:36,382
twenty-seven seconds on the build host with two OpenMP threads; this is a host-specific

167
00:18:36,382 --> 00:18:42,446
measurement rather than a promise for every computer. Evaluators can rebuild the data, retrain

168
00:18:42,446 --> 00:18:48,510
the model, rerun the tests and compare the resulting metrics. The repository also includes

169
00:18:48,510 --> 00:18:54,573
an architecture document, editable presentation, technical report, data notices, model card, video script and

170
00:18:54,573 --> 00:19:00,637
jury questions. Original application code uses the MIT license. Data and model artifacts have

171
00:19:00,637 --> 00:19:06,701
separate noncommercial attribution and share-alike terms. The leader should review and understand the implementation

172
00:19:06,701 --> 00:19:08,000
before presenting it.

173
00:19:10,000 --> 00:19:15,695
GlucoTwin India demonstrates the fusion of paired clinical and sensor streams, an evolving patient-specific

174
00:19:15,695 --> 00:19:21,390
state, working glucose forecasts, uncertainty reporting and a clinician research dashboard. The held-out experiment

175
00:19:21,390 --> 00:19:27,085
shows a modest forecast-error improvement from adaptation on three Type 2 diabetes participants. It

176
00:19:27,085 --> 00:19:32,780
also shows that the event alerts remain weak and that clinical validation is still

177
00:19:32,780 --> 00:19:38,475
required. The contribution is a functioning and auditable prototype with clear boundaries, rather than

178
00:19:38,475 --> 00:19:44,169
an unsupported claim of medical readiness. The next stage should prioritize native data, a

179
00:19:44,169 --> 00:19:49,864
larger Indian cohort, improved event modeling and prospective evaluation. Thank you for reviewing the

180
00:19:49,864 --> 00:19:55,559
project from Dipanshu Raj, Team product, IIT Kanpur. The code, results, sources and limitations

181
00:19:55,559 --> 00:19:58,000
are included in the submission package.
